-- title:
--   Positivity of the d-th coefficient of the Hilbert polynomial of L
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, let $X$ be an integral scheme with a morphism $f \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, and let $d$ be a natural number with $\operatorname{topologicalKrullDim} X = d$. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be a module over $X$ which is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ lies in an open $U$ for which the pullback of $\mathcal{L}$ along $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of $U$, and which is finite by sections over $f$: for some $N$ there are global sections $\sigma_0,\dots,\sigma_N \in \Gamma(\mathcal{L},\top)$ together with a morphism $\varphi \colon X \to \operatorname{Proj} k[X_0,\dots,X_N]$ satisfying $f = \varphi$ followed by the projection to $\operatorname{Spec} k$, such that on every open $V$ contained in $\varphi^{-1}(D(X_i))$ the map $g \mapsto g \cdot \sigma_i|_V$ is a bijection $\Gamma(X,V) \to \Gamma(\mathcal{L},V)$, and such that on $\varphi^{-1}(D(X_i))$ the pullback of the ratio $X_j/X_i$ times $\sigma_i$ equals $\sigma_j$; and $\varphi$ is required to be finite. Let $\mathcal{K}$ be a finite linearly ordered family of affine opens covering $X$. Then there is a polynomial $q \in \mathbb{Q}[T]$ whose coefficient of $T^d$ is strictly positive such that for every $m \in \mathbb{N}$ the Euler characteristic $\sum_i (-1)^i \dim_k \check H^i(\mathcal{K}, \mathcal{L}^{\otimes m})$, formed over the index range of $\mathcal{K}$ from the presheaf of sections of the $m$-th tensor power $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}$ (defined by $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes 0} = \mathbf{1}$ and $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes (n+1)} = \mathcal{L}^{\otimes n} \otimes \mathcal{L}$), equals $q(m)$ in $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   This is the Hilbert-polynomial statement for an invertible sheaf that is generated by finitely many sections defining a finite morphism to projective space: the Čech Euler characteristic of $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m}$ is given by a polynomial in $m$ whose coefficient in degree $d = \dim X$ is positive, so that $\chi(\mathcal{L}^{\otimes m})$ grows like $m^d$. It is used in the analysis of line bundles on abelian schemes and relative group laws entering the good-reduction study of Jacobians.
